# .cursorrules — Luma Scene

Project conventions for AI-assisted development (Cursor / agentic). These rules
encode how this app was built and how to keep it consistent.

## Product north star
- One job: "where are the people I follow showing up?" Don't add scope that
  dilutes that. When a feature requires a login, stop and reconsider — it
  probably doesn't belong here.
- Elegance over features. The whole point is that it's almost embarrassingly simple.

## The one architectural rule: no database, no auth
- **The URL is the account.** A user's state (cities + follows) lives entirely in
  query params: `?cities=Miami,San+Francisco&follows=usr-X,usr-Y`.
- Never introduce a database, a user table, cookies-as-identity, or a login wall
  for the core scene. If you think you need persistence, you almost certainly
  want to encode it in the URL instead.
- No private data is stored, because there is nowhere to store it. Keep it that way.

## Data: Luma's public API only
- Use Luma's public JSON endpoints (the same ones their own site calls) — no auth.
  - `api.lu.ma/user/profile/events-hosting?user_api_id=usr-…` — a host's upcoming events
  - `luma.com/user/<usr-id|handle>` — profile HTML (name + avatar)
  - `api.lu.ma/event/get?event_api_id=…` — event detail / featured guests
- A "follow" entry can arrive as a bare handle, `@handle`, full profile URL,
  short `lu.ma/u/…` URL, or a raw `usr-id`. The resolver must handle all forms.
- Never silently drop a follow. If a host can't be resolved or has no events,
  surface that ("no upcoming events") rather than making them vanish.

## Performance is a feature
- Every page render fans out to many upstream Luma calls. Two non-negotiables:
  1. Cache the built digest (`unstable_cache`, ~30 min) keyed on cities+follows so
     a refresh is an instant cache hit, not a re-crawl.
  2. Route ALL outbound Luma fetches through the rate limiter in
     `web/lib/luma-rate-limit.ts` — bounded concurrency + adaptive backoff +
     circuit breaker. Don't call `fetch` to Luma directly.
- Be a good citizen to Luma's API. Steady-state spacing should be light, but the
  adaptive backoff must stay intact so we self-throttle if Luma pushes back.

## Code conventions
- TypeScript, Next.js App Router, React Server Components. Keep data-fetching and
  digest logic server-side (`web/lib/luma.ts`); keep client components thin.
- Match the existing dark "Miami sunset" aesthetic and the design tokens in
  `web/app/globals.css` (`--ink`, `--surface-*`, `--accent*`, `--r-*`). Reuse
  tokens; don't hardcode colors or radii.
- Prefer surgical edits. Don't refactor adjacent code unless the task requires it.
- Always verify with a real build (`npm run build`) before deploying.

## Deploying
- Vercel project `luma-scene`, root is `web/`. Deploys are commit-to-`main` driven.
- Before pushing, fetch + rebase onto `origin/main` — this repo has multiple
  contributors pushing in parallel; never clobber someone else's commits.

## Don't
- Don't add a login, a database, or server-side user state.
- Don't bypass the rate limiter.
- Don't ship dev fixtures, sample-data dumps, or `.vscode/` configs in the app.
- Don't let a single broken host take down the whole scene — fail soft, per-row.
